<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gulf of Gabès</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lesser Syrtis, Syrtis Minor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabes">Gabès</a>, known in antiquity as the Lesser Syrtis, is a gulf of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> on the eastern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sfax">Sfax</a> and the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djerba">Djerba</a>. Its shallows and shifting sandbanks made it notorious among ancient sailors. The coastal trading towns around it and to the north, the Emporia, were rich prizes contested between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and Numidia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gulf-of-gabes.jpg" alt="Gulf of Gabès" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gulf_of_Gabes_NASA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The gulf spans the coast roughly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sfax">Sfax</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djerba">Djerba</a>. Sources differ on its limits. Most place its entrance between the north-western tip of Djerba and Ras Yonga, about 52 kilometres south-west of Sfax. Strabo placed it at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerkennah-islands">Kerkennah Islands</a> and Djerba, which gives a larger area about 150 kilometres across. The whole gulf lies on the continental shelf of the African Plate and is nowhere deeper than 200 metres. At its head is the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabes">Gabès</a>, where the tides range up to 2.1 metres at spring tides.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name Syrtis Minor is used by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a>, who quotes an earlier description by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> that uses the Greek equivalent. The name contrasts with the Greater Syrtis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>. Strabo gave an alternative name, the Syrtis of the lotus-eaters, and called Tacape, the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabes">Gabès</a>, an important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepôt</a> of the Lesser Syrtis.</p><p>The Syrtis is mentioned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> in the account of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-apostle">Apostle Paul</a> being sent in chains to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to stand trial before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>. The crew of his ship feared being driven by a storm into the Syrtis and took precautions, and the ship was eventually wrecked on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>. In the fourth book of his Histories Herodotus describes a violent ritual in the region, held at a festival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, in which young women divided into two camps and fought with stones and wooden clubs; those who died of their wounds were called false virgins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabes">Gabès</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sfax">Sfax</a> are both major ports on the gulf, supporting sponge and tuna fisheries, and Gabès is its economic and administrative centre. Carbonate minerals and evaporites deposited in the Permian and Triassic periods now form salt domes and diapirs beneath the gulf, and hydrocarbons deposited with them form the basis of an oil and gas industry. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Ashtart</a> and Chergui fields are among the important ones.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gulf of Gabès” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its capital was Leptis Magna, and the province stretched from the <b>Gulf of Gabès</b> to the Gulf of Sirte.</blockquote><a href="/state/tripolitania">Tripolitania</a></li></ul></section>
